  /// Any configuration errors that apply to the Next Generation policy.
  /// Network Firewall detects these errors asynchronously, after you create
  /// or update the policy, so an error can appear after the request that
  /// caused it has already succeeded. Each entry describes the problem and
  /// how to resolve it, and Network Firewall clears it automatically after
  /// you resolve the problem.
  pub errors: List[FirewallPolicyError]? = None
  /// The last time that the Next Generation policy was changed.
  pub last_modified_time: Time? = None
}

/// A single structured firewall engine rule, for use in a firewall engine
/// rule group or as an embedded rule in a Next Generation policy. A
/// firewall rule accepts, drops, or rejects traffic at a connection-state
/// hook. For more information about authoring firewall engine rules, see
/// Rule groups for a firewall using a Next Generation policy in the Network
/// Firewall Developer Guide .
pub struct FirewallRule {
  /// The action that the firewall engine takes on traffic that matches the
  /// rule. The firewall engine supports the following actions: ACCEPT - Allow
  /// the traffic. DROP - Block the traffic and discontinue inspection. REJECT
  /// - Block the traffic and send a TCP reset packet back to the client.
  /// REJECT_DST - Block the traffic and send a TCP reset packet to the
  /// destination. REJECT_BOTH - Block the traffic and send a TCP reset packet
  /// to both the client and the destination. You combine the action with an
  /// action scope, for example accept:flow . To send accepted traffic
  /// straight to its destination and skip the IPS engine, pair an accept
  /// action with the BypassIPSEnabled setting. For more information, see Rule
  /// groups for a firewall using a Next Generation policy in the Network
  /// Firewall Developer Guide .
  pub action: FirewallRuleAction? = None
  /// The scope that controls how much of the matched traffic the action
  /// applies to. The valid scopes depend on the action: ACCEPT supports all
  /// four scopes, and DROP , REJECT , REJECT_DST , and REJECT_BOTH support
  /// only PACKET and FLOW . PACKET - The current packet only. HOOK - The
  /// current hook only. The firewall engine evaluates the connection again at
  /// the next hook. TX - The current transaction. The firewall engine
  /// continues to evaluate later transactions on the connection. FLOW - The
  /// entire connection. For an accept action, the connection skips the
  /// firewall engine's remaining hooks and advances to the IPS engine. In a
  /// Suricata-compatible rule string, you write the scope in lowercase, for
  /// example accept:flow .
  pub action_scope: ActionScope? = None
  /// The connection-state hook at which the firewall engine evaluates the
  /// rule. The hook encodes both the protocol and the connection state, for
  /// example a TCP hook or a TLS client_hello hook. For the hooks that the
  /// firewall engine supports and how they map to the connection lifecycle,
  /// see Rule groups for a firewall using a Next Generation policy in the
  /// Network Firewall Developer Guide and the Suricata firewall mode
  /// documentation .
  pub hook: HookKey? = None
  /// The protocol of the rule's hook. The protocol is derived from the hook
  /// and must match the hook's protocol prefix. Network Firewall rejects a
  /// rule whose protocol doesn't match its hook.
  pub protocol: FirewallRuleProtocol? = None
  /// The source IP addresses and address ranges to inspect for, in CIDR
  /// notation. A firewall rule always compiles to a forward-direction rule,
  /// so you express inbound compared to outbound traffic through the source
  /// and destination addresses.
  pub source_ips: Str? = None
  /// The destination IP addresses and address ranges to inspect for, in CIDR
  /// notation. A firewall rule always compiles to a forward-direction rule,
  /// so you express inbound compared to outbound traffic through the source
  /// and destination addresses.
  pub dest_ips: Str? = None
  /// The source ports to inspect for.
  pub source_ports: Str? = None
  /// The destination ports to inspect for.
  pub dest_ports: Str? = None
  /// Additional options for the rule. These are the Suricata RuleOptions
  /// settings, such as the signature ID ( sid ) and match keywords.
  pub rule_options: List[RuleOption]? = None
  /// Whether Network Firewall automatically accepts the earlier
  /// application-layer hooks that a connection must pass to reach this rule's
  /// hook. Because the firewall engine is drop by default, accepting prior
  /// hooks lets you author a single rule for the hook you care about, instead
  /// of also authoring a rule for each earlier hook. This setting accepts the
  /// earlier application-layer hooks only; it doesn't accept the TCP layer.
  /// For more information, see Rule groups for a firewall using a Next
  /// Generation policy in the Network Firewall Developer Guide .
  pub accept_prior_hooks: Bool? = None
  /// Specifies whether the rule also generates an alert log for the traffic
  /// that it matches. Turning on alerting adds a secondary alert action to
  /// the rule, so that Network Firewall logs the match in addition to taking
  /// the rule's primary action.
  pub alert_enabled: Bool? = None
  /// Specifies whether an accepted connection bypasses the IPS engine. By
  /// default, traffic that the firewall engine accepts advances to the IPS
  /// engine for threat inspection. When you turn on this setting for an
  /// accept rule, the accepted traffic goes straight to its destination
  /// without IPS inspection. You can set the bypass only on accept rules, and
  /// it uses the same scope as the rule's accept action; only the flow and
  /// packet scopes support a bypass. For more information, see Rule groups
  /// for a firewall using a Next Generation policy in the Network Firewall
  /// Developer Guide .
  pub bypass_ips_enabled: Bool? = None
  /// Optional metadata for the rule: whether the rule is enabled and a
  /// description. Metadata doesn't change how the rule matches or acts on
  /// traffic.
  pub rule_metadata: RuleMetadata? = None
}

pub enum FirewallRuleAction: Str {
  Accept = "ACCEPT"
  Drop = "DROP"
  Reject = "REJECT"
  RejectDst = "REJECT_DST"
  RejectBoth = "REJECT_BOTH"
  Unknown(value: Str)
}

/// The object that defines a firewall engine rule group, for use in a Next
/// Generation policy. A firewall engine rule group provides access-control
/// rules that the firewall engine evaluates at connection-state hooks. You
/// provide the rules as a Suricata-compatible rule string, as structured
/// firewall rules, or generated from a list of domain names. For more
/// information, see Rule groups for a firewall using a Next Generation
/// policy in the Network Firewall Developer Guide .
pub struct FirewallRuleGroup {
  /// The stateful inspection criteria for the firewall engine rule group.
  pub rules_source: FirewallRulesSource? = None
  /// Settings that are available for use in the rules in the firewall engine
  /// rule group, such as IP set and port variables that the rules reference.
  pub rule_variables: RuleVariables? = None
  /// The list of a rule group's reference sets. Firewall engine rule groups
  /// don't support IP set references (reference sets); this setting applies
  /// to IPS engine rule groups. To use CIDR ranges in firewall engine rules,
  /// use rule variables instead.
  pub reference_sets: ReferenceSets? = None
}

/// A single entry in the firewall section of a Next Generation policy. An
/// entry either references an existing firewall engine rule group by ARN or
/// embeds a rule group directly in the policy, and it carries the priority
/// that sets the rule group's evaluation order within the firewall section.
pub struct FirewallRuleGroupEntry {
  /// The priority of the rule group entry within the firewall section.
  /// Network Firewall evaluates the firewall section's entries in order of
  /// increasing priority value. Each entry in the firewall section must have
  /// a unique priority.
  pub priority: Int? = None
  /// The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of an existing firewall engine rule group
  /// to reference in this entry. Provide either ResourceArn to reference an
  /// existing rule group or InlineRuleGroup to embed a rule group in the
  /// policy.
  pub resource_arn: Str? = None
  /// A firewall engine rule group defined directly in the policy, instead of
  /// referenced by ARN. Provide either InlineRuleGroup to embed a rule group
  /// or ResourceArn to reference an existing rule group. An embedded rule
  /// group can't use a domain list rules source; to filter on a domain list,
  /// reference a standalone rule group by ARN instead.
  pub inline_rule_group: FirewallRuleGroup? = None
}

pub enum FirewallRuleProtocol: Str {
  Any = "IP"
  Tcp = "TCP"
  Udp = "UDP"
  Icmp = "ICMP"
  Http = "HTTP"
  Ftp = "FTP"
  Tls = "TLS"
  Smb = "SMB"
  Dns = "DNS"
  Dcerpc = "DCERPC"
  Ssh = "SSH"
  Smtp = "SMTP"
  Msn = "MSN"
  Krb5 = "KRB5"
  Ikev2 = "IKEV2"
  Tftp = "TFTP"
  Ntp = "NTP"
  Dhcp = "DHCP"
  Http2 = "HTTP2"
  Quic = "QUIC"
  Icmpv4 = "ICMPV4"
  Icmpv6 = "ICMPV6"
  Sctp = "SCTP"
  Ipv4 = "IPV4"
  Ipv6 = "IPV6"
  Doh2 = "DOH2"
  FtpData = "FTP_DATA"
  Nfs = "NFS"
  Rdp = "RDP"
  Rfb = "RFB"
  Mqtt = "MQTT"
  Pgsql = "PGSQL"
  Snmp = "SNMP"
  Telnet = "TELNET"
  Websocket = "WEBSOCKET"
  Ldap = "LDAP"
  Pop3 = "POP3"
  Mdns = "MDNS"
  BittorrentDht = "BITTORRENT_DHT"
  Dnp3 = "DNP3"
  Unknown(value: Str)
}

/// The source of the rules for a firewall engine rule group. You provide
/// the rules as a single Suricata-compatible rule string, as a list of
/// structured firewall rules, or as a list of domain names to generate
/// rules from.
pub struct FirewallRulesSource {
  /// Firewall engine rules, provided as a single Suricata-compatible rule
  /// string in firewall mode. Use this to provide rules that you've already
  /// written in the Suricata firewall-mode format. For the rule format, see
  /// Rule groups for a firewall using a Next Generation policy in the Network
  /// Firewall Developer Guide .
  pub rules_string: Str? = None
  /// An array of individual firewall engine rules, provided as structured
  /// rules rather than as a rule string.
  pub firewall_rules: List[FirewallRule]? = None
  /// Stateful inspection criteria for a domain list, specified as the list of
  /// domain names that Network Firewall generates firewall engine rules from.
  pub rules_source_list: RulesSourceList? = None
}

/// Detailed information about the current status of a Firewall . You can
/// retrieve this for a firewall by calling DescribeFirewall and providing
/// the firewall name and ARN. The firewall status indicates a combined
/// status. It indicates whether all subnets are up-to-date with the latest
/// firewall configurations, which is based on the sync states config
/// values, and also whether all subnets have their endpoints fully enabled,
/// based on their sync states attachment values.
pub struct FirewallStatus {
  /// The readiness of the configured firewall to handle network traffic
  /// across all of the Availability Zones where you have it configured. This
  /// setting is READY only when the ConfigurationSyncStateSummary value is
  /// IN_SYNC and the Attachment Status values for all of the configured
  /// subnets are READY .
  pub status: FirewallStatusValue? = None
  /// The configuration sync state for the firewall. This summarizes the
  /// Config settings in the SyncStates for this firewall status object. When
  /// you create a firewall or update its configuration, for example by adding
  /// a rule group to its firewall policy, Network Firewall distributes the
  /// configuration changes to all Availability Zones that have subnets
  /// defined for the firewall. This summary indicates whether the
  /// configuration changes have been applied everywhere. This status must be
  /// IN_SYNC for the firewall to be ready for use, but it doesn't indicate
  /// that the firewall is ready. The Status setting indicates firewall
  /// readiness. It's based on this setting and the readiness of the firewall
  /// endpoints to take traffic.
  pub configuration_sync_state_summary: ConfigurationSyncState? = None
  /// Status for the subnets that you've configured in the firewall. This
  /// contains one array element per Availability Zone where you've configured
  /// a subnet in the firewall. These objects provide detailed information for
  /// the settings ConfigurationSyncStateSummary and Status .
  pub sync_states: Map[Str, SyncState]? = None
  /// Describes the capacity usage of the resources contained in a firewall's
  /// reference sets. Network Firewall calculates the capacity usage by taking
  /// an aggregated count of all of the resources used by all of the reference
  /// sets in a firewall.
  pub capacity_usage_summary: CapacityUsageSummary? = None
  /// The synchronization state of the transit gateway attachment. This
  /// indicates whether the firewall's transit gateway configuration is
  /// properly synchronized and operational. Use this to verify that your
  /// transit gateway configuration changes have been applied.
  pub transit_gateway_attachment_sync_state: TransitGatewayAttachmentSyncState? = None
}

pub enum FirewallStatusValue: Str {
  Provisioning = "PROVISIONING"
  Deleting = "DELETING"
  Ready = "READY"
  Failed = "FAILED"
  Unknown(value: Str)
}

/// Any number of arrays, where each array is a single flow identified in
/// the scope of the operation. If multiple flows were in the scope of the
/// operation, multiple Flows arrays are returned.
pub struct Flow {
  pub source_address: Address? = None
  pub destination_address: Address? = None
  /// The source port to inspect for. You can specify an individual port, for
  /// example 1994 and you can specify a port range, for example 1990:1994 .
  /// To match with any port, specify ANY .
  pub source_port: Str? = None
  /// The destination port to inspect for. You can specify an individual port,
  /// for example 1994 and you can specify a port range, for example 1990:1994
  /// . To match with any port, specify ANY .
  pub destination_port: Str? = None
  /// The protocols to inspect for, specified using the assigned internet
  /// protocol number (IANA) for each protocol. If not specified, this matches
  /// with any protocol.
  pub protocol: Str? = None
  /// Returned as info about age of the flows identified by the flow
  /// operation.
  pub age: Int? = None
  /// Returns the total number of data packets received or transmitted in a
  /// flow.
  pub packet_count: Int? = None
  /// Returns the number of bytes received or transmitted in a specific flow.
  pub byte_count: Int? = None
}

/// Defines the scope a flow operation. You can use up to 20 filters to
/// configure a single flow operation.
pub struct FlowFilter {
  pub source_address: Address? = None
  pub destination_address: Address? = None
  /// The source port to inspect for. You can specify an individual port, for
  /// example 1994 and you can specify a port range, for example 1990:1994 .
  /// To match with any port, specify ANY .
  pub source_port: Str? = None
  /// The destination port to inspect for. You can specify an individual port,
  /// for example 1994 and you can specify a port range, for example 1990:1994
  /// . To match with any port, specify ANY .
  pub destination_port: Str? = None
  /// The protocols to inspect for, specified using the assigned internet
  /// protocol number (IANA) for each protocol. If not specified, this matches
  /// with any protocol.
  pub protocols: List[Str]? = None
}

/// Contains information about a flow operation, such as related statuses,
/// unique identifiers, and all filters defined in the operation. Flow
/// operations let you manage the flows tracked in the flow table, also
/// known as the firewall table. A flow is network traffic that is monitored
/// by a firewall, either by stateful or stateless rules. For traffic to be
/// considered part of a flow, it must share Destination, DestinationPort,
/// Direction, Protocol, Source, and SourcePort.
pub struct FlowOperation {
  /// The reqested FlowOperation ignores flows with an age (in seconds) lower
  /// than MinimumFlowAgeInSeconds . You provide this for start commands.
  pub minimum_flow_age_in_seconds: Int? = None
  /// Defines the scope a flow operation. You can use up to 20 filters to
  /// configure a single flow operation.
  pub flow_filters: List[FlowFilter]? = None
}

/// An array of objects with metadata about the requested FlowOperation .
pub struct FlowOperationMetadata {
  /// A unique identifier for the flow operation. This ID is returned in the
  /// responses to start and list commands. You provide to describe commands.
  pub flow_operation_id: Str? = None
  /// Defines the type of FlowOperation .
  pub flow_operation_type: FlowOperationType? = None
  /// A timestamp indicating when the Suricata engine identified flows
  /// impacted by an operation.
  pub flow_request_timestamp: Time? = None
  /// Returns the status of the flow operation. This string is returned in the
  /// responses to start, list, and describe commands. If the status is
  /// COMPLETED_WITH_ERRORS , results may be returned with any number of Flows
  /// missing from the response. If the status is FAILED , Flows returned will
  /// be empty.
  pub flow_operation_status: FlowOperationStatus? = None
}

pub enum FlowOperationStatus: Str {
  Completed = "COMPLETED"
  InProgress = "IN_PROGRESS"
  Failed = "FAILED"
  CompletedWithErrors = "COMPLETED_WITH_ERRORS"
  Unknown(value: Str)
}

pub enum FlowOperationType: Str {
  FlowFlush = "FLOW_FLUSH"
  FlowCapture = "FLOW_CAPTURE"
  Unknown(value: Str)
}

/// Describes the amount of time that can pass without any traffic sent
/// through the firewall before the firewall determines that the connection
/// is idle and Network Firewall removes the flow entry from its flow table.
/// When you update this value, existing connections will be treated
/// according to your stream exception policy configuration.
pub struct FlowTimeouts {
  /// The number of seconds that can pass without any TCP traffic sent through
  /// the firewall before the firewall determines that the connection is idle.
  /// After the idle timeout passes, data packets are dropped, however, the
  /// next TCP SYN packet is considered a new flow and is processed by the
  /// firewall. Clients or targets can use TCP keepalive packets to reset the
  /// idle timeout. You can define the TcpIdleTimeoutSeconds value to be
  /// between 60 and 6000 seconds. If no value is provided, it defaults to 350
  /// seconds.
  pub tcp_idle_timeout_seconds: Int? = None
}

pub enum GeneratedRulesType: Str {
  Allowlist = "ALLOWLIST"
  Denylist = "DENYLIST"
  Rejectlist = "REJECTLIST"
  Alertlist = "ALERTLIST"
  Unknown(value: Str)
}

