/// The policy-level configuration for resolved IP validation. When you set
/// this on a firewall policy, you require that the destination IP address
/// of a connection is one that the Amazon Route 53 Resolver returned in a
/// DNS response for a domain. The Route 53 Resolver must have returned the
/// response in a VPC that's associated with the Route 53 Resolver DNS
/// Firewall rule group identified by DNSFirewallRuleGroupId . At the policy
/// level, this verifies that the destination IP address was resolved for
/// some domain. To match specific domains, also configure stateful rule
/// groups that use the AWS_ROUTE_53_RESOLVED_DOMAIN target type or the
/// aws_route_53_resolved_domain rule keyword. Enabling or disabling
/// resolved IP validation, or changing DNSFirewallRuleGroupId , may require
/// a restart of the stateful engine in order to apply the change. When this
/// occurs, existing connections will be treated according to your stream
/// exception policy configuration. Changes to Action ,
/// DNSExpiryExceptionPolicy , SourceIPRanges , and
/// BypassDestinationValidationIPRanges don't require a restart.
pub struct Route53ResolvedIPValidation {
  /// The action that Network Firewall takes for a connection whose
  /// destination IP address fails resolved IP validation. A destination IP
  /// address fails validation when the Route 53 Resolver didn't return it in
  /// a DNS response in an associated VPC. Valid values are the following:
  /// ALERT – Tags the matching connection in the firewall's flow logs
  /// without blocking it, when you enable logging. Use this to observe the
  /// effect of resolved IP validation before you enforce it. DROP –
  /// Silently blocks the connection without sending any response to the
  /// client. REJECT – Blocks the connection and sends a TCP reset (RST) to
  /// the client. Because a TCP reset applies only to TCP connections, Network
  /// Firewall drops non-TCP traffic instead of resetting it.
  pub action: Route53ResolvedIPValidationAction? = None
  /// The ID of the Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall rule group that determines
  /// which VPCs' DNS resolutions count for resolved IP validation. A
  /// destination IP address counts as resolved for a domain only when the
  /// Route 53 Resolver returned it in a DNS response within a VPC that's
  /// associated with this rule group. Associate the rule group with the VPCs
  /// that send traffic to the firewall. The ID must match the pattern
  /// ^rslvr-frg-[0-9a-f]+$ . It must also exist and be accessible from your
  /// account. Network Firewall validates this when you create or update the
  /// policy. Changing this value swaps the managed rule group association.
  /// Network Firewall processes the swap asynchronously. Each policy can have
  /// only one managed Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall association. While a
  /// swap is in progress, Network Firewall rejects further changes to this
  /// setting. Wait until the swap completes, then retry. The change may also
  /// require a restart of the stateful engine. When this occurs, existing
  /// connections will be treated according to your stream exception policy
  /// configuration.
  pub dns_firewall_rule_group_id: Str? = None
  /// The action that Network Firewall takes when the DNS resolution recorded
  /// for a destination IP address has expired from the resolved IP cache.
  /// This applies when Network Firewall still sees traffic to that IP address
  /// after the expiry. Network Firewall keeps a resolution for longer than
  /// the DNS record's time to live (TTL). Valid values are the following:
  /// CONTINUE – Treats the expired resolution as still valid and doesn't
  /// block the connection because of expiry. DROP – Silently blocks traffic
  /// to the IP address after its resolution has expired, without sending any
  /// response to the client. REJECT – Blocks traffic to the IP address
  /// after its resolution has expired and sends a TCP reset (RST) to the
  /// client. Network Firewall drops non-TCP traffic instead of resetting it.
  pub dns_expiry_exception_policy: DNSExpiryExceptionPolicy? = None
  /// The destination IP address ranges that are exempt from the policy-level
  /// resolved IP check. Network Firewall doesn't apply the Action in this
  /// configuration to a connection whose destination falls in these ranges,
  /// even if the Route 53 Resolver didn't return that IP address in an
  /// associated VPC. Specify each entry as an IPv4 or IPv6 address or CIDR
  /// range, up to 100 entries. This exemption applies only to the
  /// policy-level check. Stateful rules that use the
  /// AWS_ROUTE_53_RESOLVED_DOMAIN target type or the
  /// aws_route_53_resolved_domain keyword still evaluate connections to these
  /// destinations and match them independently of this list.
  pub bypass_destination_validation_ip_ranges: List[Address]? = None
  /// The client source IP address ranges whose connections are subject to
  /// resolved IP validation. Specify each entry as an IPv4 or IPv6 address or
  /// CIDR range, up to 100 entries. If you omit this field, resolved IP
  /// validation applies to the CIDR ranges of the firewall's VPC and of any
  /// additional VPCs attached to the firewall. Specifying an empty list is
  /// not the same as omitting the field: an empty list means that Network
  /// Firewall doesn't validate any source addresses. This scope applies to
  /// the policy-level check only. Stateful rules that use the
  /// AWS_ROUTE_53_RESOLVED_DOMAIN target type or the
  /// aws_route_53_resolved_domain keyword still evaluate their destinations
  /// regardless of SourceIPRanges .
  pub source_ip_ranges: List[Address]? = None
}

pub enum Route53ResolvedIPValidationAction: Str {
  Reject = "REJECT"
  Drop = "DROP"
  Alert = "ALERT"
  Unknown(value: Str)
}

