/// The stateless or stateful rules definitions for use in a single rule
/// group. Each rule group requires a single RulesSource . You can use an
/// instance of this for either stateless rules or stateful rules.
pub struct RulesSource {
  /// Stateful inspection criteria, provided in Suricata compatible rules.
  /// Suricata is an open-source threat detection framework that includes a
  /// standard rule-based language for network traffic inspection. These rules
  /// contain the inspection criteria and the action to take for traffic that
  /// matches the criteria, so this type of rule group doesn't have a separate
  /// action setting. You can't use the priority keyword if the RuleOrder
  /// option in StatefulRuleOptions is set to STRICT_ORDER .
  pub rules_string: Str? = None
  /// Stateful inspection criteria for a domain list rule group.
  pub rules_source_list: RulesSourceList? = None
  /// An array of individual stateful rules inspection criteria to be used
  /// together in a stateful rule group. Use this option to specify simple
  /// Suricata rules with protocol, source and destination, ports, direction,
  /// and rule options. For information about the Suricata Rules format, see
  /// Rules Format on the Suricata website.
  pub stateful_rules: List[StatefulRule]? = None
  /// Stateless inspection criteria to be used in a stateless rule group.
  pub stateless_rules_and_custom_actions: StatelessRulesAndCustomActions? = None
}

/// Stateful inspection criteria for a domain list rule group. For HTTPS
/// traffic, domain filtering is SNI-based. It uses the server name
/// indicator extension of the TLS handshake. By default, Network Firewall
/// domain list inspection only includes traffic coming from the VPC where
/// you deploy the firewall. To inspect traffic from IP addresses outside of
/// the deployment VPC, you set the HOME_NET rule variable to include the
/// CIDR range of the deployment VPC plus the other CIDR ranges. For more
/// information, see RuleVariables in this guide and Stateful domain list
/// rule groups in Network Firewall in the Network Firewall Developer Guide
/// .
pub struct RulesSourceList {
  /// The domains that you want to inspect for in your traffic flows. Valid
  /// domain specifications are the following: Explicit names. For example,
  /// abc.example.com matches only the domain abc.example.com . Names that use
  /// a domain wildcard, which you indicate with an initial ' . '. For
  /// example, .example.com matches example.com and matches all subdomains of
  /// example.com , such as abc.example.com and www.example.com .
  pub targets: List[Str]? = None
  /// The types of matching to perform against the domains in Targets . You
  /// can specify any combination of the following:
  /// AWS_ROUTE_53_RESOLVED_DOMAIN – Match the domain against the domain
  /// that the Amazon Route 53 Resolver resolved the connection's destination
  /// IP address for. HTTP_HOST – Match the domain against the Host header
  /// of HTTP traffic. TLS_SNI – Match the domain against the Server Name
  /// Indication (SNI) of HTTPS traffic. AWS_ROUTE_53_RESOLVED_DOMAIN matches
  /// on how the destination IP address was resolved, rather than on the
  /// domain that the client asserts in its TLS SNI or HTTP Host header. This
  /// target type takes effect when you configure resolved IP validation on
  /// the firewall policy. For more information, see
  /// Route53ResolvedIPValidation . Whether you include
  /// AWS_ROUTE_53_RESOLVED_DOMAIN changes what the generated rules match,
  /// together with GeneratedRulesType : Combining
  /// AWS_ROUTE_53_RESOLVED_DOMAIN with TLS_SNI or HTTP_HOST in an ALLOWLIST
  /// requires both conditions to match. The SNI or Host header must match the
  /// domain, and the Route 53 Resolver must have resolved the destination IP
  /// address for that domain. Network Firewall allows traffic only in that
  /// case. Traffic that matches one condition, such as a spoofed Host header
  /// sent to an unresolved IP address, is not allowed. In a DENYLIST , or
  /// when AWS_ROUTE_53_RESOLVED_DOMAIN is the only target type, Network
  /// Firewall evaluates each condition independently: a match on either the
  /// domain (SNI or Host ) or the resolved destination IP address triggers
  /// the rule. When you don't include AWS_ROUTE_53_RESOLVED_DOMAIN , the
  /// generated rules match only on the SNI or Host header, without checking
  /// how the destination IP address was resolved. An ALLOWLIST whose only
  /// target type is AWS_ROUTE_53_RESOLVED_DOMAIN is additive: it allows
  /// matching traffic but doesn't generate a default rule that drops
  /// non-matching traffic. Only the TLS_SNI and HTTP_HOST target types
  /// generate that default drop. To allow only the resolved domains and drop
  /// all other traffic, include a protocol target type as well.
  pub target_types: List[TargetType]? = None
  /// Whether you want to apply allow, reject, alert, or drop behavior to the
  /// domains in your target list. When logging is enabled and you choose
  /// Alert, traffic that matches the domain specifications generates an alert
  /// in the firewall's logs. Then, traffic either passes, is rejected, or
  /// drops based on other rules in the firewall policy.
  pub generated_rules_type: GeneratedRulesType? = None
}

/// Any Certificate Manager (ACM) Secure Sockets Layer/Transport Layer
/// Security (SSL/TLS) server certificate that's associated with a
/// ServerCertificateConfiguration . Used in a TLSInspectionConfiguration
